# Normalise line endings in the repository, and leave the working tree alone.
#
# `* text=auto` makes git normalise to LF in the index and check out with the
# platform's own convention; `eol=lf` overrides that and checks out LF everywhere.
# The second one is the deliberate choice here, for three reasons:
#
#   - The repository has a Windows CI leg, and a maintainer on Windows. With
#     `text=auto` alone, the same file has different bytes on disk depending on
#     who checked it out, and a diff that shows every line as changed is the
#     symptom.
#   - `src/index.js` is a `bin`, with a shebang. A CRLF shebang is `/usr/bin/env
#     node\r` on Linux and macOS: "bad interpreter", and the installed package
#     does not start. `scripts/verify-package.mjs` now asserts the file has no
#     `\r` for exactly this reason.
#   - Every generated artefact — the tarball, a lockfile diff, a `.tgz` checksum —
#     is byte-sensitive, and CRLF is the kind of difference that is invisible in
#     review and visible in a checksum.
#
# WHY THE BINARY EXCEPTIONS ARE HERE
# ----------------------------------
# The rule is `text` and not `-text`, so git decides by inspection. That is right
# for a repository with no binary files, and the two entries below are not
# belt-and-braces: they are there so that a `.tgz` or a `.node` added later is not
# line-ending-damaged on its way into the index. A tarball mended by git is a
# tarball npm cannot install.
#
# A NOTE ON THE FIVE CRLF FILES ALREADY IN THE TREE
# --------------------------------------------------
# Five of the forty-three tracked files are CRLF in the index today
# (`CHANGELOG.md`, `package-lock.json`, `package.json`, `src/adapters/mongodb.js`,
# `src/core/schema.js`) and thirty-eight are LF. This file does not fix that, on
# purpose: `git add --renormalize .` rewrites every tracked file, which turns a
# one-line configuration change into a whole-repository diff and buries the real
# change. It has to be a separate commit, on its own, so that `git log -p` on the
# next change is readable:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "chore: normalise line endings to LF"
#
# until that commit exists, treat this file as the *policy* and the index as still
# holding the old state.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.js   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.cjs  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Not text, and never to be line-ending-processed.
*.tgz    binary
*.node   binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary

# Lockfiles and tarballs are generated and compared byte for byte.
package-lock.json text eol=lf
